- House-hunting weekend negotiation, themes
    Summary: Buyer chronicles a failed asking-price negotiation, retaliatory cuts, walkaway, and lessons on realtor leverage. (¶1, ¶18, ¶19)
        - 1 Theme: Year-long search in a split market with N, J, C, and L. (¶1)
            - 1.1 Evidence: Many houses linger very long; some vanish in 1-2 days. (¶1)
            - 1.2 Evidence: N buyer's realtor; J seller's realtor; C partner; L assistant. (¶1)
        - 2 Theme: First Wednesday offer at affordability ceiling still fails. (¶2, ¶4)
            - 2.1 Evidence: Highest affordable bid sits about 30K below asking price. (¶2)
            - 2.2 Evidence: Thursday 6pm rejection; sellers proceed with other offers. (¶4)
        - 3 Theme: Friday asking-price bid seeks instant acceptance before rivals. (¶5, ¶6)
            - 3.1 Evidence: Friday 1pm showing convinces; sweet asking-price offer planned. (¶3, ¶5)
            - 3.2 Nuance: N advises 10K below to leave negotiation room; buyer unconvinced. (¶5)
            - 3.3 Evidence: Friday 6pm offer at asking with sweets, deadline midnight same day. (¶6)
        - 4 Theme: Seller side cites busyness, seeks extension, mentions rival offer. (¶7, ¶8) [reported]
            - 4.1 Evidence: Friday 10:30pm: driving up island; extend to tomorrow midnight; another offer mentioned. (¶8) [reported]
            - 4.2 Nuance: Buyer doubts the rival offer and fears bid-war leverage. (¶8)
            - 4.3 Evidence: Friday 11pm counter: extension only to 10:30am Saturday. (¶9)
        - 5 Theme: Buyer retaliates with cuts; sellers counter above asking. (¶11, ¶13)
            - 5.1 Evidence: Saturday 5pm: buyer drops 20K from asking, strips sweets, defying N. (¶11)
            - 5.2 Evidence: J replaced by C on REW, gone from 4 listings; $3.5M showing noted. (¶12)
            - 5.3 Evidence: Saturday 9pm counter 5K above asking with disclosure; reply by 11pm. (¶13) [reported]
            - 5.4 Nuance: Saturday silence, rare nap as load lifts; N later endorses cuts. (¶10, ¶14)
        - 6 Theme: Sunday email fight ends cooperation; buyer demands in-person terms. (¶16, ¶17)
            - 6.1 Evidence: L calls sub-2-hour Sunday deadline unprofessional; cites 37 years admin experience. (¶16) [reported]
            - 6.2 Rebuttal: N replies Friday full-price stall left unwanted 2nd and 3rd offers. (¶16) [reported]
            - 6.3 Evidence: Sunday 8am: 10K below asking, no sweets, 11am deadline; believes rival ceiling is 45K-30K below. (¶15)
            - 6.4 Nuance: Buyer refuses further open offers; wants in-person Monday after 5:30. (¶17, ¶18)
        - 7 Theme: House sells below asking; buyer sets disclosure rules. (¶18, ¶19)
            - 7.1 Evidence: Two weeks later sold 10K below asking; N owes coffee. (¶19)
            - 7.2 Evidence: New rule: no volunteered offer talk; written disclosure letters required. (¶18)
            - 7.3 Example: Earlier house resisted 5-10% push; sold 5% over bid after 7 months. (¶20)
            - 7.4 Nuance: Seller trains car dealerships; realtor system plays clients as pawns. (¶20, ¶21)
        - 8 Theme: Claude debrief: February 2025, 3-day Friday-Sunday talks, walked away. (¶22) [reported]
            - 8.1 Evidence: Arc: optimistic start, skeptical middle, detached strategic end. (¶22) [reported]
            - 8.2 Evidence: Tactics: disclosure demands, short windows, stripped sweets, no bidding war. (¶23) [reported]
            - 8.3 Evidence: Successes: bluff detection, discipline; failures: no purchase, strained ties. (¶24) [reported]
            - 8.4 Nuance: Keep verification; soften timelines and style; add walkaway thresholds. (¶24) [reported]
